    "knowledge_point": "Determining the Convergence of Sequences Containing Exponential Logarithmic Function",
    "problem": "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}{{e}^{3n}}$ for $n\\ge 1?$",
    "worked_solution": "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit, we calculate\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}{{e}^{3n}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}{{e}^{3n}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges, and its limit is $0.$",
